, serviceable_pct, arpu, share_pct, and target_pct are each individually stated and challengeable. |\n| 21 | Formulas shown | PASS | sizing.json includes formula strings for each TAM/SAM/SOM figure for both top-down and bottom-up approaches. Intermediate steps are captured in inputs. |\n| 22 | Sources cited | PASS | All external data points have source attribution in validation.json: IBISWorld (market size, HVAC/plumbing/electrical firm counts), MarketsandMarkets (growth rate), FieldServiceSoftware.io (ARPU reference). URLs, publishers, quality_tier, and date_accessed provided for each. |\n\n## Methodology\n\n**Approach:** Both (top-down and bottom-up cross-validation)\n**Rationale:** Industry analyst reports exist for the field service management software market, enabling a top-down approach. Granular data on contractor firm counts (US Census / BLS) and realistic ARPU benchmarks from public SaaS comparables enable a credible bottom-up calculation. Using both approaches provides cross-validation, which is more convincing to investors at the seed stage.\n\n## Definitions\n\n- **TAM** (Total Addressable Market): Total market demand for the product/service if 100% market share were achieved.\n- **SAM** (Serviceable Available Market): The segment of TAM targeted by your products and services that is within your geographical reach.\n- **SOM** (Serviceable Obtainable Market): The portion of SAM that you can realistically capture in the near term.\n\n## Market Sizing\n\n**Top-down:** Starting from industry total of $3.1B, targeting 30.0% segment with 3.0% market share.\n\n**Bottom-up:** 504,000 potential customers x $4.5K ARPU, 45.0% serviceable, 3.0% target capture.\n\n| Metric | Value | Method | Provenance | Key Assumptions |\n|--------|-------|--------|------------|-----------------|\n| TAM | $3.1B | Top-down | sourced | Industry Total: $3.1B |\n| SAM | $930.0M | Top-down | derived | TAM: $3.1B, Segment %: 30 |\n| SOM | $27.9M | Top-down | agent_estimate | SAM: $930.0M, Market Share %: 3 |\n| TAM | $2.3B | Bottom-up | derived | Customer Count: 504,000, ARPU: $4.5K |\n| SAM | $1.0B | Bottom-up | derived | Serviceable Customers: 226,800, Serviceable %: 45, ARPU: $4.5K |\n| SOM | $30.6M | Bottom-up | agent_estimate | Target Customers: 6,804, Target Capture %: 3, ARPU: $4.5K |\n\n**Cross-validation:** TAM delta = 31.0%. Top-down and bottom-up TAM differ by 31.0% (>30%). Review assumptions \u2014 one approach likely has a flawed input.\n\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- **US Field Service Management Software Market (2026)** = $3.1B (Sourced)\n- **SMB Trades (HVAC/Plumbing/Electrical) Share of US FSM Market** = 30 (Derived)\n- **Cadence Target Market Share (3\u20135 Year SOM)** = 3 (Estimate)\n- **Total US HVAC + Plumbing + Electrical Contractor Firms** = 504,000 (Sourced)\n- **Average Annual Revenue Per Customer (Blended SaaS ARPU)** = $4.5K (Derived)\n- **Serviceable Contractor Firms (SMB with SaaS Appetite)** = 45 (Derived)\n- **Cadence Target Capture Rate (SOM vs SAM)** = 3 (Estimate)\n- **Market Growth Trajectory** = 1 (Sourced)\n\n## Validation\n\n- **US FSM Software Market Size**: validated (2 sources)\n- **US HVAC + Plumbing + Electrical Contractor Firms**: validated (2 sources)\n- **Annual Revenue Per Customer**: partially_supported (1 source)\n- **SMB Trades Share of US FSM Market**: partially_supported (1 source)\n- **Serviceable Fraction of Contractor Firms**: partially_supported (1 source)\n\n## Sensitivity Analysis\n\nThe table below shows how each market tier changes when each assumption moves between its low and high estimate. Parameters tagged *Estimate* have wider ranges because they lack external sourcing \u2014 they tend to dominate the sensitivity, which highlights exactly where better data would most strengthen the analysis.\n\n| Parameter | Approach | Confidence | Low Value | Base Value | High Value | Low TAM | Base TAM | High TAM | Low SAM | Base SAM | High SAM | Low SOM | Base SOM | High SOM | Range |\n|-----------|----------|------------|-----------|------------|----------|---------|----------|----------|---------|----------|----------|---------|----------|----------|-------|\n| Industry Total | Top-down | Sourced | $2.6B | $3.1B | $3.7B | $2.6B | $3.1B | $3.7B | $790.5M | $930.0M | $1.1B | $23.7M | $27.9M | $33.5M | [-15.0%, +20.0%] |\n| Segment % | Top-down | Derived | 21% | 30% | 39% | $3.1B | $3.1B | $3.1B | $651.0M | $930.0M | $1.2B | $19.5M | $27.9M | $36.3M | [-30.0%, +30.0%] |\n| Market Share % | Top-down | Estimate | 1.5% | 3% | 4.5% | $3.1B | $3.1B | $3.1B | $930.0M | $930.0M | $930.0M | $13.9M | $27.9M | $41.9M | [-50.0%, +50.0%] |\n| Customer Count | Bottom-up | Sourced | 453,600 | 504,000 | 554,400 | $2.0B | $2.3B | $2.5B | $918.5M | $1.0B | $1.1B | $27.6M | $30.6M | $33.7M | [-10.0%, +10.0%] |\n| ARPU | Bottom-up | Derived | $3.1K | $4.5K | $6.3K | $1.6B | $2.3B | $3.2B | $714.4M | $1.0B | $1.4B | $21.4M | $30.6M | $42.9M | [-30.0%, +40.0%] |\n| Serviceable % | Bottom-up | Derived | 31.5% | 45% | 58.5% | $2.3B | $2.3B | $2.3B | $714.4M | $1.0B | $1.3B | $21.4M | $30.6M | $39.8M | [-30.0%, +30%] (widened) |\n| Target Capture % | Bottom-up | Estimate | 1.5% | 3% | 6% | $2.3B | $2.3B | $2.3B | $1.0B | $1.0B | $1.0B | $15.3M | $30.6M | $61.2M | [-50.0%, +100.0%] |\n\n**Most sensitive parameter:** Target Capture %\n\n## Warnings\n\n- [~] **TAM Discrepancy:** Top-down and bottom-up TAM differ by 31.0% (>30%) [Accepted: Top-down TAM ($3.1B) uses the full US FSM software market as the universe, then filters to SMB trades (30%); bottom-up TAM ($2.27B) counts only HVAC/plumbing/electrical firms directly. The 31% delta is a scope difference, not a flawed assumption \u2014 the SAMs converge within 9% ($930M vs $1.02B), confirming underlying alignment. Both figures are internally consistent.]\n- [!!!] **Checklist Failures:** Checklist has 1 failures: ['som_backed_by_gtm']\n\n## Sources Used\n\n- [Field Service Management Software in the US Market Size Statistics, 2026](https://www